---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import { loadManifest } from '@data/loadSnapshot';

const { ruleset } = loadManifest();
---

<BlogPostLayout slug="how-to-use-the-wow-forever-talent-calculator">
  <p>
    The calculator on this site does a small number of things: it lets you
    spend talent points under the same rules the game uses, it keeps a few
    builds on your device, and it turns a build into a link. Most of the
    confusion we hear about comes from the parts that look obvious, such as
    what the level box really controls or why a talent will not accept a
    point. This walkthrough covers each part in the order you are likely to
    meet it.
  </p>

  <h2>Start from a class page</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/">home page</a> opens with a class switcher and a working
    calculator. Every class also has its own address, for example
    <a href="/druid/">/druid/</a> or <a href="/warrior/">/warrior/</a>, which
    is the page to bookmark if you only play one class. Each class has three
    talent trees laid out as a grid of seven rows and up to four columns.
    The level box defaults to {ruleset.defaultLevel}, and the counter beside
    it shows points left against the total for that level. At level
    {ruleset.maxLevel} the total is {ruleset.maxBudget}.
  </p>
  <p>
    Changing the level changes the budget, not the talents. If you spend 20
    points and then drop the level below the one those points require, the
    calculator will not let you lower it. The line next to the counter, which
    reads "Needs at least level N", tells you the minimum level for what you
    have already spent. Our <a href="/blog/wow-forever-talent-points-levels-and-row-gates/">explainer on points, levels and row gates</a>
    goes through the arithmetic.
  </p>

  <h2>Adding and removing points</h2>
  <p>On a desktop browser the controls are mouse and keyboard:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Click a talent to add a point.</li>
    <li>Right-click it, or hold Shift and click, to remove a point.</li>
    <li>
      Use the arrow keys to move between talents and the + and - keys to add
      or remove a point without the mouse.
    </li>
    <li>
      Escape closes the detail view. Undo, Redo and Reset all sit above the
      trees, and each tree has its own reset.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On a phone the trees become tabs. Tapping a talent selects it and opens
    a detail panel with Add and Remove buttons, which avoids accidental
    points from scrolling. The same panel is available on desktop and is the
    quickest way to see why a talent is unavailable.
  </p>
  <p>
    A talent can refuse a point for four reasons: the tree has not received
    enough points in earlier rows, a prerequisite talent is missing, the talent
    is already at max rank, or you have no points left at that level. The
    detail panel lists each requirement and marks it as met or not met with
    your current numbers. Removal works the other way around. You cannot take
    a point out of a talent if another talent you have taken depends on it,
    or if doing so would break the row requirement of something further down.
    The panel names the talents that block removal, so you know what to
    remove first.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading ranks and evidence badges</h2>
  <p>
    The detail panel shows the effect text for your current rank and for the
    next one. Under each block is a line starting with "Source", which
    states how much we know about that specific rank: for example "Community
    preview record" or "Effect unknown". A small question mark on a talent
    in the tree means the effect for the rank you are looking at is not
    known. The full list of statuses and what each one claims is on the
    <a href="/sources/">Sources page</a>, and
    <a href="/blog/where-wow-forever-talent-data-comes-from/">this post on where the data comes from</a>
    explains how far to trust each label.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some talents also carry a "Classic reference" block. It shows what the
    Classic version looked like, whether the talent is unchanged, changed,
    moved or new, and the tree and row it came from. That block is a
    comparison aid for people who remember the old trees; it is not part of
    the rules for your build.
  </p>

  <h2>Saving a build</h2>
  <p>
    Type a name in the build name field and press Save build. The build is
    stored in your browser's local storage on this device, up to 50 named
    builds, and appears on the <a href="/my-builds/">My Builds</a> page.
    From there you can open, rename, delete or export a build as JSON, and
    import a JSON file or pasted text back in. Because nothing is uploaded,
    a saved build does not follow you to another browser or device, and
    clearing site data removes it. If you want a copy that survives a browser
    change, export the JSON or keep a share link.
  </p>
  <p>
    Separately from named builds, the calculator keeps a draft for each class
    so that refreshing the page does not lose your work. If your browser
    blocks local storage, for example in some private modes, the page shows
    a notice and everything else keeps working in memory.
  </p>

  <h2>Sharing a build</h2>
  <p>
    The Share button creates a link of the form
    <code>/druid/#b=...</code> and copies it to the clipboard, or shows it in
    a field you can copy by hand. The text after <code>#b=</code> is the
    build itself: class, level, data snapshot and the rank in each talent.
    It does not include a build name or anything about you. Anyone who
    opens the link sees your build in read-only mode, with a banner that says
    so, and can press Edit a copy to start changing their own version. Until
    they do, the banner promises that their local draft is left alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Links record the data snapshot they were made on. If we have since
    updated the data, an older link still opens: renamed talents keep their
    points, points in talents that no longer exist are refunded, and a note
    on the page says what changed. The <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>
    lists those data updates. If a link does not open at all, the page shows
    the reason and the original link as plain text, so nothing is lost.
  </p>

  <h2>Comparing two builds</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/compare/">Compare</a> page takes two builds, each chosen
    from your saved builds or pasted as a share link, and reports tree totals
    for each plus every talent whose rank differs. Both builds must be the
    same class and on the same data snapshot, otherwise the page tells you
    why it cannot compare them. It is most useful for answering a narrow
    question, such as "what exactly did I move when I switched from build A
    to build B", and less useful as a ranking tool, because it only reports
    differences, not which side is better.
  </p>

  <div class="post-callout">
    <h3>A short checklist before you share a build</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check the level: the link carries it, and the viewer sees it.</li>
      <li>
        Open the link yourself in a private window to confirm it loads
        read-only as you expect.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you are posting it publicly, mention the date or the data build,
        because talents can change between beta builds.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2>What the calculator does not do</h2>
  <p>
    It does not simulate damage, model gear, or tell you which build to pick.
    It does not know about Legacy perks in the standard budget either, so
    the point total is the one earned by leveling. If you want a tool for
    those questions you will need another source; if you want to know what
    a talent does according to the data we have, how many points it takes to
    reach it, and what you give up by taking it, this is what the calculator
    is for. Problems or mismatches with the game are welcome on the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
